      "id": "classical-style-balance-and-sonata-form",
      "title": "Classical Style: Balance and Sonata Form",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** styles | **Difficulty:** 4/10 | **Time:** ~10 min"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Classical era (c. 1750–1820) values symmetry, homophonic texture, and clearly articulated phrases."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "texture",
      "title": "Texture",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Melody with accompaniment dominates over baroque polyphony. Alberti bass (broken chords) supports singable top lines."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "sonata-form",
      "title": "Sonata form",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Exposition (themes in home and dominant keys) → development (fragmentation, modulation) → recapitulation (themes in home key). Large-scale question-answer logic applies to short tunes too."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ensemble",
      "title": "Ensemble",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Haydn and Mozart string quartets model conversational listening — metaphor for good session etiquette."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "entities": [],
